   "last_activity_utc": "2019-05-15T19:14:25Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Yes, the code has a memory leak unless you delete the pointers. If the <code>foo</code> class owns the pointers, it is its responsibility to delete them. You should do this before clearing the vector, otherwise you lose the handle to the memory you need to de-allocate.</p>\n\n<pre><code>   for (auto p : v)\n   {\n     delete p;\n   } \n   v.clear();\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>You could avoid the memory management issue altogether by using a <code>std::vector</code> of a suitable <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_pointer\" rel=\"noreferrer\">smart pointer</a>.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 12795228,
   "parent_id": 12795196,
   "author_user_id": null,
   "author_display_name": "user349026",
   "author_id_missing": true,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/12795228",
   "score": 13,
   "created_utc": "2012-10-09T07:48:36Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2012-10-09T08:01:10Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-10-09T08:01:10Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Nope you only clear the vector storage. Allocated memory with 'new' is still there.</p>\n\n<pre><code>for (int i =0; i&lt; v.size();i++)\n   {\n     delete (v[i]);\n   } \n   v.clear();\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 17300279,
   "parent_id": 12795221,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 859227,
   "author_display_name": "mahmood",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "asker",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/12795196/#comment17300279_12795221",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2012-10-09T07:52:16Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "can you please give me straghit method (not the smart ptr)??"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 12795299,
   "parent_id": 12795196,
   "author_user_id": 1303277,
   "author_display_name": "tozka",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/12795299",
   "score": 7,
   "created_utc": "2012-10-09T07:52:54Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2012-10-09T07:52:54Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>You can use <code>for_each</code> :</p>\n\n<pre><code>std::vector&lt;int*&gt; v;\n\ntemplate&lt;typename T&gt;\nstruct deleter : std::unary_function&lt;const T*, void&gt;\n{\n  void operator() (const T *ptr) const\n  {\n    delete ptr;\n  }\n};\n\n// call deleter for each element , freeing them\nstd::for_each (v.begin (), v.end (), deleter&lt;int&gt; ());\nv.clear ();\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 17300294,
   "parent_id": 12795228,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": null,
   "author_display_name": "user349026",
   "author_id_missing": true,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/12795196/#comment17300294_12795228",
   "score": 1,
   "created_utc": "2012-10-09T07:52:56Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "use &#39;delete&#39; on each iteration. After than use clear to clear the vector. e,g delete(*it)"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
